          <div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div>
          <div class="of-ftype__content">
            <div class="of-ftype__step">05</div>
            <h2 class="of-ftype__title">Anti-Graffiti Film.
Protect. Peel. Replace.</h2>
            <p class="of-ftype__desc">Anti-graffiti window film is a clear sacrificial layer applied to glass, mirrors, and polished metal surfaces. When vandalized — scratched, acid-etched, or spray-painted — only the film is replaced, not the glass. Particularly relevant for Center City offices, ground-floor lobbies, elevator interiors, and any high foot-traffic Philadelphia location.</p>
            <div class="of-ftype__faq">
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How is it different from regular window film?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Anti-graffiti film is optically clear with no tint or solar control properties — its sole purpose is to protect the surface beneath it. It's applied as a sacrificial layer specifically designed to be peeled off and replaced after vandalism, saving the cost of glass or mirror replacement.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can it be applied to elevator interior panels?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes — anti-graffiti film works on polished stainless steel, mirrors, and architectural metal surfaces in addition to glass. Elevator interiors, restroom mirrors, and lobby feature walls are common applications in Philadelphia commercial buildings. We stock multiple thicknesses for different surface types.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How long does replacement take?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Film replacement after vandalism typically takes 30–60 minutes per panel depending on size. We keep stock on hand for quick-turnaround replacement jobs for our regular commercial clients. Building managers appreciate the fast turnaround versus waiting for glass replacement.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Get an anti-graffiti estimate →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SLIDE 06: Low-E & Climate Control -->
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="5">
          <img src="/images/office-ftype-lowe.jpg" alt="Warm cozy Philadelphia office interior in winter with energy-efficient Low-E window film keeping heat inside" class="of-ftype__bg" />
          <div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div>
          <div class="of-ftype__content">
            <div class="of-ftype__step">06</div>
            <h2 class="of-ftype__title">Low-E Film.
Warm in Winter. Cool in Summer.</h2>
            <p class="of-ftype__desc">Low-emissivity (Low-E) window film works year-round: in summer it rejects incoming solar heat; in winter it reflects interior warmth back inside rather than letting it radiate through the glass. The 3M Thinsulate CC75 is the benchmark for Philadelphia buildings with older single-pane windows looking to improve insulation without full window replacement.</p>
            <div class="of-ftype__faq">
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How does Low-E film differ from solar control film?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Solar control film is optimized to reject heat in summer but doesn't significantly improve winter insulation. Low-E film has a low emissivity coating that also reduces heat loss in winter — reflecting interior radiant heat back into the room. For Philadelphia's four-season climate, Low-E is often the better choice for older single-pane buildings.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does it change how the glass looks?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>3M Thinsulate CC75 is essentially clear — it adds a very slight neutral appearance that's barely noticeable in most settings. Unlike reflective solar films, it doesn't create a mirrored exterior look. Views remain clear in both directions.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does it work on double-pane glass?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Some Low-E films can be applied to the interior surface of IGU (insulated glass unit) windows with caution — we assess the glass type, age, and seal condition before recommending. For double-pane units in good condition, we typically recommend spectrally selective solar control film rather than Low-E to avoid potential thermal stress.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Get a Low-E film estimate →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>

